foreign  advertising for 15 years before you  started writing can  a bit about what made you switch from  advertising to your career in writing  uh well I got fired about five or six  times from advertising jobs about every  18 months during my career I'd have to  do the math to sort of figure it out  completely but  um I sort of stumbled into advertising  by mistake and it's a complete waste of  a life so I just was desperate to get  out and  uh I never thought I really could write  a book but I finally did write a book  and then it got a couple of big advances  and I just walked out one day and like  nobody was sad to see me go  so that was my career in advertising  sure and you mentioned your first book  about How I Live Now and I won all these  incredible Awards um did you expect this  I don't think I expected anything really  I mean I  well I sent it off to my agent and she  said oh my God this book is brilliant  and I I came home and I said oh they're  so nice in publishing and I didn't  realize that actually they're not that  nice but she really liked the book so in  lots of ways I kind of got lucky you  know that the first book was a big hit  and then everyone sort of  assumes that you're good at what you do  so it's a little bit easier after that  um what was your reaction when well I  had been diagnosed with breast cancer  the week the book came out  [Music]  and so and the awards came after that so  I was in the middle of treatment and all  that kind of stuff and it was fantastic  because it meant that I didn't have to  do all the pr stuff which actually I  would have enjoyed but I didn't have the  energy to do it  um but no one really expected anything  of me and all these kind of nice reviews  kept coming in and I thought God this is  so easy why didn't I become a writer  before  and you've written for young adults and  children um what about writing for  younger audiences appeals to you and  what does it offer as an adult you say I  mean I've written for adults as well and  in fact I think in a way I'm always  writing for adults but I'm interested in  adolescence as a life stage and so  um  I'm not so much writing  for teenagers but I'm writing about that  period of life and I have a kind of  theory that adolescence lasts from about  the age of you know 11 until  you know whenever did you actually grow  up which in my generation nobody ever  grows up because baby boomers all think  they're still 25.  um but it's you know the thing about  being a teenager is you're interested in  certain questions you're interested in  who am I what am I going to do with my  life will anybody ever love me and those  are kind of questions that just sort of  continue through your life on and off  you know if you have a girlfriend you're  probably not worried about is anyone  ever going to love me but you know six  months from now when she dumps you  you might start thinking again  which I believe  in dressage yes  there well I was I was doing a lot of  horse riding because it was kind of my  dream as a child and something I was  writing books and I had a bit of extra  money and a bit of extra time so I  thought okay I'm gonna try to fulfill my  dream of riding horses and I sort of  figured it was a big expensive waste of  time but then as I  I wasn't very good at it either but as I  did it more and more and more  I started to kind of get into the lingo  of dressage I'm when they talk about  threwness they talk about a cycle of  energy that propels the horse in a way  that  is incredibly powerful so most horses  you know they just kind of plot along  but a dressage horse pulls itself up  like a ballet dancer  and um and I started to think what if  the what if the rider  was  the conscious mind and the horse was the  unconscious mind so the horse is this  big fifteen hundred pound  you know animal and you can't control it  by Brute Force you have to control it by  you know softness and making it willing  to cooperate with you and so then I  started thinking about the consciously  unconscious minds and how all that  applies to writing that's a very brief  version of my lecture on throughness and  on that topic of Consciousness one  writing I read that it was whilst  walking your dogs so you met a  baddington terrier called Mila yes and  uh your opening line for picture me gone  yeah um do everyday happenings and kind  of subconscious moments often Inspire  your writing not often enough but when  they do happen they're unbelievably  exciting and they you know they do  happen sometimes and they feel  incredibly magical when they when they  do and then you feel like something is  happening in a deep part of your brain  that you're not really in control of  um but you know what you want is for  them to happen every day and they just  don't and what can you do to try and get  yourself into that well yeah I mean I  talk about that a lot and you know it's  sort of like you know when you go to bed  and you shut your conscious mind off and  then your unconscious mind starts  telling stories and people don't even  think about how weird that is but you  know it's not dreaming about what I will  do at work tomorrow it's dreaming about  a deer in a tree and you know in a  hunter but he's not really a hunter he's  actually a fish and so there's all this  kind of symbolic storytelling that  happens in your in your unconscious  brain  and it's about trying to access that and  so you can do it when you're asleep you  can do it when you're half asleep  um you know sometimes just when you're  walking thinking about other things you  know uh it just it just happens but it's  kind of hard to predict sadly  to know whether a story is  really really really good question  um  how do you know if a story is worth  telling I mean I've dreamed  the opening line of some of my books and  there's just something about it that  that resonates that you know and I don't  usually have the story in my head I I  mean the book I read for adults was  called Jonathan Unleashed and I woke up  one morning with the line  Jonathan came home from work one day to  find the dogs talking about him and I  didn't know who Jonathan was and I  didn't know who the dogs were or whether  they were really talking to him or  whether he was just hallucinating but I  just somewhere knew that that was the  opening of a book  now whether that's complete self you  know delusion and any old line could be  the beginning of a book I don't know  and what advice would you give to  someone who wants to be a writer he's  not sure whether they have anything  important to say  I mean  I certainly didn't think I had anything  important to say at all you know you  find out by doing  um you don't know if you can paint a  picture until you actually set up the  canvas and get the paints out and it  doesn't happen overnight  um you know you have to  yeah you have to really want to do it  because it's not easy I mean working a  bank isn't easy either and working  advertising was hellish but at least you  knew if you showed up between nine and  five they would give you some money  whereas with writing you know you can  show up between nine and five every  single day for two years and no one  gives you any money so  you know I don't really have advice I  think if you feel you have to do it then  you do it  can you tell us a little bit about your  latest book The Great Garden what  inspired it and what did you learn about  yourself while writing it oh my God  um what inspired it was I sold I was at  a Book Festival in Norfolk  years ago and they did a sudden round  robin where they said oh we're raising  money for I can't remember what it was  for to keep the Book Festival going and  could you donate a character in one of  your books so what you do is you say  okay  for the highest bidder I will name a  character after you so this woman called  blank Garden  bought the character in my book whether  she ever paid the money I don't even  have any idea  and then I had to write a book with her  as the main character and then I finally  in fact it took about 10 years but I  finally wrote the book and I sent it to  her and she said oh well I'm too famous  now so I don't want you to use my name  so I changed the first name  and then she said I'd quite like you to  change the name Garden as well because  it's not very common and I just kind of  said  sorry you know  and I didn't and it turned out to be a  great name because it has all those you  know the great Garden that sounds a  little bit like circusy sounds a little  bit like Gatsby it sounds you know God I  mean there's just like lots of  resonances so in a way it was a  coincidental book  s  well I hope there is one in the future I  mean you just don't ever know really I  mean every time I'm between books I  think that's it my career is over I'm  never going to make another penny I'm  never going to write another book and  I'm kind of at that stage now  um so we'll see  thank you very much thank you  [Music]  for more debates talks and interviews  subscribe today to The Institute of Art  and ideas at IAI TV

